[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-27
TITLE: Input Signals
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware has 5 inputs which represent five different physical buttons which the system user
can press. These five inputs are debounced by sampling the state of the input signals every time a
processing flag gets set. This processing flag is set every 1 msec. Once an input displays a toggled
value, the firmware begins a debounce countdown. During this debounce period, any bouncing
signals are ignored. After the debounce period is over, the firmware checks to see if the input truly
did toggle or if it was just noise. If the signal indicates a “true” toggle, the firmware updates the
changed button input states for processing and reporting to the main control application firmware.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-6
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-11
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-28
TITLE: Output Signals
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware controls two digital output signals. One digital output signal controls the output state
of the illumination LEDs. The second digital output signal controls the output state of the laser
modules.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-5
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-29
TITLE: Magnetometers
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware periodically reads two external magnetometers on the same I2C bus. Both
magnetometers are of the same make and model but vary in their I2C addresses. The firmware
alternates between reading each magnetometer. The X, Y, and Z values of the magnetometer are
read and subsequently sent to the main control board for the data to be sent to the PC for
treatment plan parameter compensation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-10
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-30
TITLE: Flow Sensor
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware monitors the flow sensor digital input signal once every millisecond (based on a flag
that is set inside of a time-based interrupt firing once every millisecond). The firmware checks for a
toggling state of the flow sensor input. Based on the number of signal toggles within a fixed amount
of time, the flow rate is calculated.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-12
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-31
TITLE: Pressure Sensor
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware reads and converts the analog signal from the pressure sensor using the internal
ADC of the microcontroller.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-13
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-32
TITLE: Temperature Sensor
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware reads a thermistor temperature sensor to determine the water temperature. The
reading is performed by the internal ADC on the microcontroller. The reading value is converted
into a temperature using a lookup table and interpolation to derive the approximate intermediate
values between lookup values. While the actual thermistor conversion is not linear, each step is
small enough where linear interpolation is good enough.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-1
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-33
TITLE: Control Communication Interface
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the main control board via one of the microcontroller’s serial
UART interfaces. The firmware both receives and transmits data on this UART port. Due to the
nature of UART having independent transmit and receive lines, the transmit and receive
functionality operate independently. As the UART baud rate is relatively slow compared to the
processing speed of the microcontroller, both sending and receiving data is performed with nonblocking
interrupt callback functions.

Transmitted data is sent as a buffer of bytes, representing a singular data packet to the PC, with
the interrupt callback occurring once all data bytes have been transmitted. The firmware sets a flag
when transmission is initiated, and clears the flag after transmission is finished, so as to not create
transmission conflicts on the transmit line.

Reception is performed one byte at a time, with the interrupt callback occurring once a data byte is
available. The parsing of the received byte is done to determine whether a complete data packet
has been received from the PC, as according to the communication protocol. Once a completed
data packet has been received, the firmware sets a flag for further data processing outside of the
parsing inside of the interrupt callback.

As transmission on UART is asynchronous both in terms of clock as well as word alignment (within
a packet), a “sync value” is utilized at the start of each packet to help synchronize communication
in case of any lost or missing bytes.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-8
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-34
TITLE: Control Communication Data
STATEMENT: >>>
The data received from the main control board is the desired indicator LED output, representing the
current state of the system. As this is a simple single byte value, it is sent by the main control board
multiple times in a single data packet, to help the head interface board firmware to detect any
transmission errors.

The transmitted data to the head interface board are cooling system feedback values monitored at
the system’s x-ray head, as well as the reported values of other x-ray head peripherals such as the
state of the buttons, and the values from the magnetometers and collimator. All values are reported
in 4-byte fields separated by delimiters. As the collimator ID value is a 64-bit value, there are two 4-
byte fields which together represent the 64-bit ID.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-2
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-35
TITLE: ADC Peripheral
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware utilizes the internal ADC of the microcontroller to measure the pressure and
temperature sensors. Each signal is on a separate ADC channel. The firmware continuously
alternates between each channel to get new readings.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-1
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-13
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-36
TITLE: Buttons
STATEMENT: >>>
After monitoring for debouncing, the firmware also performs specific actions based on which
buttons were pressed. The following actions are mapped to specific button presses:

-Laser button release results in the laser output signal toggling (alternating between lasers on and
off)

-LED button release results in the illumination LED output signal toggling (alternating between
illumination LEDs on and off)
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-5
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FHI-38
TITLE: Indicator LED Data
STATEMENT: >>>
As the firmware implements an indicator LED interface witch its individual colors for individual
sections of the indicator LED ring, the data is structured in such a way so that the indicator LEDs
can display specific “sequences” or “patterns”. These patterns are each structs, which are then all
organized into an array of such patterns. Each pattern is indexed according to a specific system
state (that is, each animation pattern is tied to a specific state).

The animation struct contains two components: an integer indicating the number of “frames” in the
animation, and an array of multiple “frames”. In turn, each “frame” is itself a struct. A frame is
comprised of two components: an integer value indicating the millisecond duration of the frame,
and a two-dimension array indicating the colors of the frame.

The color array of each frame is two dimensional since each frame not only has multiple sectors
(corresponding to the physical quadrants of the indicator LED ring), but also each sector is
represented by the values of three individual LEDs (red, green, and blue).

When the indicator LED interface is outputting data to the LED controllers, it will cycle through the
current animation pattern to output the current frame. It does so by setting the output of the LED
controllers to the specified colors of the frame, for the given duration of the frame. Once the frame
time has expired, the LED interface moves onto the next frame of the current pattern.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FHI-6
  ROLE: Refines

